Week 2 Lesson Notes — Cryptographic Hashing & Integrity

## 1. Core Concept

Explain the foundational concept clearly and practically.
Hashing is the process of taking any amount of data and running it through an algorithm to get a unique, fixed-length "digital fingerprint" called a hash. The most important rule of hashing is Integrity: if the data changes by even one single bit, the hash changes completely. It is a one-way street; you can turn a cow into a burger (hash), but you can't turn the burger back into a cow (original data).

---

## 2. Why It Matters

It matters because if the information gets intercepted, we have to know if it's been altered. 

---

## 3. Technical Breakdown

A hash function takes an input and produces a fixed-length alphanumeric string. 
A file is run through a hash algorithm (e.g. SHA-256)
It produces a unique fingerprint 
That fingerprint is stored or transmitted alongside the file
The recipient runs the same hash on the file they received
If the two fingerprints match, the file is intact. If they differ, it was tampered with.

---

## 4. Common Misconceptions
Hashing is encryption — it is not. Encryption is reversible with a key. Hashing is one-way and produces a fingerprint, not a locked version of the data.
A small change produces a small hash change — wrong. Even a single character change produces a completely unrecognizable hash. There is no proportional relationship between the size of the change and the difference in output.

---

## 5. Where This Shows Up

PKI / Certificate signatures — the CA hashes the certificate contents and encrypts that hash with their private key to create the digital signature. Your browser re-hashes the certificate and compares to verify nothing was tampered with.

